{
	admin 0.0.0.0:2019
	# Belune renders its own per-domain HTTP→HTTPS redirect (the ForceHTTPS
	# subroute in proxy/caddy/routes.go). Caddy's automatic redirect routes
	# would shadow it and ignore the per-domain toggle, so suppress them.
	auto_https disable_redirects
	# Optional extra global options, injected per-environment. The dev stack sets
	# this to `local_certs` so Caddy issues from its own internal CA: dev
	# hostnames (*.belune.local) have no public DNS, so a real ACME challenge
	# could only ever fail — repeatedly, against Let's Encrypt's rate limits.
	# Unset in production, where the default public ACME issuer is what we want.
	{$BELUNE_CADDY_GLOBAL_OPTIONS}
	# Expose Caddy's own runtime metrics at /metrics on the admin API
	# (scraped by Prometheus in the observability stack).
	servers {
		metrics
	}
}

# Default site — Belune dashboard (not logged — internal traffic only).
#
# This declares :80 only, yet srv0 ends up listening on :443 as well. The
# HTTPS listener is added at runtime by the API (proxy/caddy.ensureServer),
# deliberately rather than here: the Caddyfile adapter emits one server per
# port, so writing `:80, :443` would split this into srv0=:443 + srv1=:80 and
# quietly strand every dynamically-added route on the wrong server.
:80 {
	reverse_proxy api:8080
}

# Per-app configs are managed dynamically via the Caddy Admin API.
# Access logging is configured by the PaaS app at startup via the Admin API.
# See: infra/caddy/sites/
import sites/*
